  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 1010 AM MDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nStarting the extended period Friday, high pressure continues to\nremain parked across the SW CONUS. Guidance suggests that a \nshortwave moves off of the northern Rockies. The better forcing \nis currently forecast to remain to the north which puts \nprecipation potential a little in question but currently \ncounties along and north of Highway 36 currently appears to have\nthe relative higher potential.\n\nInto the weekend a bit more pronounced trough is seen moving \ninto the area from the Intermountain West. This is forecast to \nshift the high further south across the southern Plains. This \nmay lead to a bit more active pattern across the area. The \nquestion will be how far south will the trough sag and if it \nwill allow vorticity maxima to move across the area which \ncurrently appears to be the only forcing at this time as the \nbetter jet dynamics are currently forecast to remain off to the \nnorth. A similar pattern then may continue throughout next week \nas well but the high elongates some and has some indications of \nshifting a little east. If this does occur then an increase in \nmonsoonal moisture may develop as this would then become a \nclassic pattern daily showers and storms. \n\nAs for temperatures for the period. As long as the high \npressure remains nearby the area then temperatures would remain \nin the upper 80s to mid 90s. If cloud cover were to remain thick\nenough then temperatures would have to be knocked down around \n5-10 degrees but it will be dependent on precipitation coverage \nthrough the day.",
